  "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{e}^{y} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{y}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({e}^{y}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $x=-2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=-2$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{e}^{y} & =5 \\\\ 4+{e}^{y} & =5 \\\\ {e}^{y} & =1 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{0} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
